# FigDown — figures as text. Spec: https://github.com/FigDown/figdown

# Reference figure for genre `sequence` — EXPERIMENTAL GENRE, and one step
# further out than the others: this genre HAS NO RENDERER YET. The document
# below parses to a complete model and reports zero errors, and drawing it
# produces an empty canvas. That is why there is NO sequence.svg beside this
# file: an artifact would record a drawing the language does not have.
#
# What the file is for today is therefore the MODEL, not the picture. It
# demonstrates every form of every keyword the genre has, so a reader can see
# the whole surface on one page and a coverage tool can count it.
#
# EVERY line below is outside the compatibility promise: the genre is
# experimental (`CONSTRUCT-STATUS-TIERS`'s rule, applied to `sequence`). Read
# spec/genres/experimental/sequence.md first — when it exists; until then the
# ruled surface is decisions/registry.md and the reading rule is
# read/0.4/experimental/sequence.md.
figdown 0.4 sequence
title "Address lease and renewal"

# `class` declares a meaning ONCE and elements join it with class=. Under this
# genre it carries two jobs no keyword does, because both keywords were
# refused: it is what `group` would have expressed (a set of participants with
# something in common — `SEQUENCE-PARTICIPANT-GROUPING`) and what `lost=` would have expressed (a message
# that was sent and not delivered — `UNDELIVERED-MESSAGE-MARKING`). A class earns a legend entry, so
# the meaning reaches the reader without a spelling of its own.
class dropped "Sent, never delivered"

# ---- lifeline: the participant column -----------------------------------
# `lifeline` is this genre's NODE spelling — the slot `node` fills in block and
# `state` fills in statechart (UML 2.5.1 §17.3.3.1). Column order is
# DECLARATION order and there is no key that changes it: `flow` and `rank` are
# not keywords of this genre, because both axes here are already ordered by the
# source and a key that reordered the drawing would make the picture disagree
# with the text.
# Label optional, exactly as on `node`: `lifeline c` is legal and displays "c".
lifeline c "DHCP client"
lifeline s "DHCP server"

# ---- message: the line between two participants --------------------------
# `message` is this genre's CONNECTOR spelling (UML 2.5.1 §17.4.4.1) and it
# takes all three operators. The trailing quoted string is the message text.
# `--` is a LINE ERROR here and legal in every other genre: a Message has a
# sending AND a receiving event occurrence, so a direction-less message is not
# a thing this domain has.
message c -> s "DHCPDISCOVER"
message c <- s "DHCPOFFER"
message c -> s "DHCPREQUEST"
message c <- s "DHCPACK"

# ---- state: a named state OF a lifeline ----------------------------------
# Slot 1 REFERENCES a lifeline; it does not declare an id, because nothing in
# this genre refers to a state occurrence. That is the one asymmetry with
# `statechart`'s `state`, where slot 1 declares. Slot 2 is the state name and
# is MANDATORY — a state occurrence with no name asserts nothing.
state c "BOUND"

# ---- fragment + operand: the combined fragment and its compartments -------
# `type=` is MANDATORY and its value is one of the twelve UML
# InteractionOperatorKind literals taken whole. UML defaults the attribute to
# `seq`; FigDown does not, because a default draws a frame that looks like an
# assertion and is not one.
fragment renew "renewal cycle" type=loop
# An `operand` is a compartment OF a fragment, so its `in=` is MANDATORY. The
# quoted string is the guard.
operand t1 "at T1 — 0.5x the lease" in=renew

# Membership is `in=`, sense 1: the element this one lives inside. Members must
# be CONTIGUOUS in declaration order, because an operand denotes the ORDERED
# RUN of the occurrences it contains.
message c -> s "DHCPREQUEST (unicast, renewing)" in=t1 class=dropped description="the issuing server does not answer, which is what makes T2 fire"

# `in=` on a `state` too (five acceptors: message, operand, lifeline, state,
# fragment). A state occurrence can sit inside a fragment.
state c "RENEWING" in=t1

# ONE level of fragment nesting: this fragment sits inside an operand of the
# fragment above, and no deeper. Two levels is a line error in v1 — the v0.1
# `group` precedent, and a scope decision rather than a principle.
fragment fallback "if T1 expires" type=alt in=t1
operand t2 "at T2 — 0.875x the lease" in=fallback
message c <-> s "DHCPREQUEST (broadcast, rebinding)" in=t2

# `in=` on a `lifeline` is the fifth acceptor. It is legal and it is rare: it
# says a participant exists only inside a fragment. Not used here, because
# both participants exist for the whole exchange — and a reference figure
# demonstrates forms that are true of the figure, never forms invented to fill
# a table. The form is exercised by conformance fixture 285 instead.
